  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127829,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 254,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Multiple scattered, tightly packed lobules of capillaries are present throughout the dermis and (more rarely) the subcutis, in a cannonball pattern. The lumina of vascular channels are often difficult to discern, and pericytes are prominent. The endothelial cells are flat and occasionally contain intracytoplasmic hyaline structures. A distinctive feature is the presence of a crescent-like lymphatic channel around many individual tumour lobules. The stroma between tumour lobules may be desmoplastic and contain lymphatic-like channels. Focal fascicles of spindled endothelial cells, which are positive for podoplanin (recognized by D2-40), CD31, ERG, and CD34, may be seen within the capillary lobules.</p>\r\n<h6>Differential diagnosis</h6>\r\n<p>Nodular Kaposi sarcoma lacks a cannonball architecture and is positive for KSHV/HHV8.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:01:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
